I apologize.

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:19.859
So network functions,
we talked a little bit

00:08:19.859 --> 00:08:21.150
about the global load balancer.

00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:22.900
I want to talk about
it a little bit more.

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:28.020
So with a global network,
at those pop locations,

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:31.590
we're able to provide
load balancers that

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:34.919
present a single global
IP to all of your clients

00:08:34.919 --> 00:08:36.179
across the world.

00:08:36.179 --> 00:08:39.360
So one VIP with
the same IP address

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:40.505
serving the entire globe.

00:08:40.505 --> 00:08:41.880
And the way that
we're able to do

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:45.450
that is we leverage a technology
called anycast, which really

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:47.340
means that we're
advertising the same IP

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:49.320
addresses at all
of our locations.

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:53.609
And we've IP'd your VIP
with that same IP address

00:08:53.609 --> 00:08:54.900
at each one of those locations.

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:57.930
What this does is it attracts
traffic from our peers

00:08:57.930 --> 00:09:02.550
to the most geographically
proximate ingress location.

00:09:02.550 --> 00:09:07.170
And then from there, we
back haul that to whatever

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:09.450
cloud back end you
have serving it.

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:12.690
This enables you to do
global load balancing, again,

00:09:12.690 --> 00:09:18.630
without manipulating DNS or in
fact taking sometimes signals

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:21.667
from DNS that are
potentially inaccurate.

00:09:24.590 --> 00:09:26.880
So this slide is a bit
of a bit of a mess.

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:30.407
So I'm going to try and
walk you through it.

00:09:30.407 --> 00:09:32.740
What we have here-- this is
an engineering conversation,

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:33.240
right?

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:36.400
You weren't expecting really
beautiful slides, were you?

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:41.740
So anyway, we're going to start
from, I guess, your right side

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:43.600
and walk it towards me.

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:49.420
So what we've got here
is a basic example

00:09:49.420 --> 00:09:52.210
of a VPN using our
global network.

00:09:52.210 --> 00:09:55.670
And again, this is about
choice and ease of use.

00:09:55.670 --> 00:09:58.120
So let's say you've got
your on-prem network

00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:01.619
and you've got this green VPN
termination device at your end.

00:10:01.619 --> 00:10:03.160
You're going to
traverse the internet

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:05.550
with your IPsec tunnel.

00:10:05.550 --> 00:10:07.690
And you're going to land
that at a VPN gateway

00:10:07.690 --> 00:10:10.570
in a cloud region.

00:10:10.570 --> 00:10:12.620
So when it lands in
that cloud region,

00:10:12.620 --> 00:10:14.710
you've got some choices.

00:10:14.710 --> 00:10:19.810
You can, first of all, just use
resources in that cloud region

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:22.600
and route back to that
VPN termination point

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:24.070
through that tunnel.

00:10:24.070 --> 00:10:27.130
Or you can export the routes
using our cloud router

00:10:27.130 --> 00:10:32.830
and also a toggle
called Global Routes

00:10:32.830 --> 00:10:35.710
to route all the information
that your project learns

00:10:35.710 --> 00:10:38.277
from the VPN globally.

00:10:38.277 --> 00:10:39.610
And that's just a simple toggle.

00:10:39.610 --> 00:10:42.820
So you can either isolate
your VPN endpoint locally,

00:10:42.820 --> 00:10:45.220
or you can or you can
enable it globally.

00:10:45.220 --> 00:10:50.260
Likewise you can advertise
your routes from Google,

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:54.610
that you learned from
Google, back across that VPN

00:10:54.610 --> 00:11:00.320
connection, including any routes
that your VPC has globally.

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:03.990
And you do that with a
mechanism called Custom Routes.

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:06.190
And this is just
a small example.

00:11:06.190 --> 00:11:09.490
Also, I've got in
there a layer 4 ILB.

00:11:09.490 --> 00:11:12.460
The interesting thing about
that is that you can literally

00:11:12.460 --> 00:11:17.080
use an internal load balancer at
the far end of a VPN connection

00:11:17.080 --> 00:11:20.470
to load balance
internal traffic,

00:11:20.470 --> 00:11:21.800
again, anywhere in the world.

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:22.690
And we're going to
talk a little bit more

00:11:22.690 --> 00:11:24.065
about how that
works in a minute.

00:11:29.140 --> 00:11:32.790
So the network load
balancer is interesting.

00:11:32.790 --> 00:11:34.720
This is more of a
regional construct.

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:36.860
But the interesting thing
about the network load

00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:39.140
balancers, it actually
uses our Edge pop

00:11:39.140 --> 00:11:42.440
as well because this is for
public internet traffic coming

00:11:42.440 --> 00:11:47.060
into a cloud region, but it's
not load balance at layer 7.

00:11:47.060 --> 00:11:49.940
So when we talk about the global
load balancer, or the Layer 7

00:11:49.940 --> 00:11:51.740
VIP, that does termination.

00:11:51.740 --> 00:11:54.110
So it actually terminates
an HTTPS connection.

00:11:54.110 --> 00:11:57.410
It terminates an SSL
connection or it proxies TCP.

00:11:57.410 --> 00:12:02.480
This is actually a layer
3, layer 4 load balancer

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:07.565
that directs traffic to two
backends in a cloud region,

00:12:07.565 --> 00:12:09.540
a specific cloud region.

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:12.470
So if you wanted to load
balance across multiple regions,

00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:15.230
in this case, you would
actually deploy load balancers

00:12:15.230 --> 00:12:18.520
in each region that you
wanted to load balance.

00:12:18.520 --> 00:12:22.100
And so in this situation,
we've got Maya in California,

00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:24.680
Bob in London, and
Shan in Singapore.

00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:27.820
And a couple of the interesting
things about this load

00:12:27.820 --> 00:12:31.280
balancing capability is that
we do preserve the client IP

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:34.010
address so that we can
do direct server return.

00:12:34.010 --> 00:12:37.970
And also, it facilitates
creating firewall rules

00:12:37.970 --> 00:12:39.350
on the cloud backend.

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:40.470
This is kind of important.

00:12:40.470 --> 00:12:43.340
Again, to make things easy,
you have the same IP address

00:12:43.340 --> 00:12:47.910
on your LB VIP, as you
do on your backend VIP.

00:12:47.910 --> 00:12:50.600
And so it's just
one firewall rule

00:12:50.600 --> 00:12:57.390
at the regional level to manage
security for those connections.

00:12:57.390 --> 00:12:59.730
Another regional function--
and this one is more truly

00:12:59.730 --> 00:13:00.580
regional--

00:13:00.580 --> 00:13:01.830
is the internal load balancer.

00:13:01.830 --> 00:13:06.520
We talked about it a little
bit in the case of the VPN,

00:13:06.520 --> 00:13:10.440
but sort of how it
works is, again, it

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:12.610
allows you to create
an internal event.

00:13:12.610 --> 00:13:14.130
But in this case,
it's completely

00:13:14.130 --> 00:13:17.230
programmed in a distributed
fashion across our Andromeda

00:13:17.230 --> 00:13:18.150
infrastructure.

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:20.250
There are no middle
boxes in this case.

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:23.490
In the other cases, the
global load balancer,

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:26.850
there's a middle box
that's doing termination.

00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:28.770
In the network
load balancer case,

00:13:28.770 --> 00:13:31.390
there's a middle box that's
actually that's actually

00:13:31.390 --> 00:13:32.640
directing traffic at the edge.

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:35.130
It's something we call a Maglev.

00:13:35.130 --> 00:13:38.130
Internally, in these
internal load balancers,

00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:40.680
this is completely using the
distributed software defined

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:43.500
network that we've built. And
it's actually programming flows

00:13:43.500 --> 00:13:45.330
directly onto each host.

00:13:45.330 --> 00:13:48.720
And there are no middle
boxes to speak of.

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:51.010
Again, client IP
addresses are preserved.

00:13:51.010 --> 00:13:53.760
It's easy to set it's
easy to control access

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:56.610
with firewall rules.

00:13:56.610 --> 00:14:01.020
And we believe it offers
higher performance because

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:01.820
of no choke points.

00:14:01.820 --> 00:14:06.090
Again, no middle boxes for
these kinds of load balancers.

00:14:06.090 --> 00:14:09.770
So let's talk a little
bit about how it works.

00:14:09.770 --> 00:14:14.090
So again, we talked
about that global VPC.

00:14:14.090 --> 00:14:18.700
So remember, we've got to do
functional and performance

00:14:18.700 --> 00:14:19.460
isolation.

00:14:19.460 --> 00:14:23.920
And what that means is,
again, every project in cloud

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:27.130
is using the same
infrastructure globally,

00:14:27.130 --> 00:14:30.700
but you're all operating
on different or distinct

00:14:30.700 --> 00:14:35.950
isolated network name spaces.

00:14:35.950 --> 00:14:39.070
So we all have overlapping
IP addresses basically.

00:14:39.070 --> 00:14:41.770
But you're also all
expecting us to provide

00:14:41.770 --> 00:14:43.210
performance guarantees.

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:45.670
And so we also have
to do isolation

00:14:45.670 --> 00:14:47.600
at the performance level.

00:14:47.600 --> 00:14:50.860
And then finally, we have
to offer high availability

00:14:50.860 --> 00:14:51.840
to everyone.

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:53.440
So our high
availability targets--

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:55.960
and this is interesting--

00:14:55.960 --> 00:14:58.250
we as a mnemonic called 3, 4, 5.

00:14:58.250 --> 00:15:01.570
So if you deploy an
application in a single zone,

00:15:01.570 --> 00:15:05.740
our target for availability
is three 9s of availability.

00:15:05.740 --> 00:15:09.670
If you deploy across two
zones in the same region,

00:15:09.670 --> 00:15:12.490
or regionally as
we would call it,

00:15:12.490 --> 00:15:15.400
then you would expect
four 9s of availability.

00:15:15.400 --> 00:15:16.930
And if you deploy
something globally

00:15:16.930 --> 00:15:20.290
across multiple regions,
you should expect five 9s

00:15:20.290 --> 00:15:22.690
of availability on our network.

00:15:22.690 --> 00:15:24.460
We think, number
one, that's unique.

00:15:24.460 --> 00:15:27.440
But, number two, that creates a
whole bunch of problems for us,

00:15:27.440 --> 00:15:30.100
since if your application
availability needs

00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:32.230
to be 3, 4, 5, that means
that what we're deploying

00:15:32.230 --> 00:15:33.370
is actually 4, 5, 6.

00:15:37.710 --> 00:15:42.090
So a unified view
of functions, this

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:48.070
is where I go to the note cards,
so bear with me for one second.

00:15:48.070 --> 00:15:50.710
So where are we?

00:15:53.960 --> 00:15:57.260
OK, so we wanted to spend
some time on the data plane.

00:15:57.260 --> 00:16:00.920
And basically, the
message behind this slide

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:04.400
is that we've built the
core network functions

00:16:04.400 --> 00:16:06.440
into the VM host themselves.

00:16:06.440 --> 00:16:11.134
And that's a consistent theme
across the Andromeda network.

00:16:11.134 --> 00:16:12.800
There's a constant
evolution of features

00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:14.940
that we push out weekly.

00:16:14.940 --> 00:16:18.770
And we can do this because
we support live migration

00:16:18.770 --> 00:16:20.630
so that we can do
host maintenance

00:16:20.630 --> 00:16:22.310
in a non-disruptive fashion.

00:16:22.310 --> 00:16:25.589
And also, we can do hitless
upgrades of our data plane.

00:16:25.589 --> 00:16:27.130
And we'll talk a
little bit about how

00:16:27.130 --> 00:16:29.390
we can do that as well.

00:16:29.390 --> 00:16:31.600
So the architecture
itself, first of all,

00:16:31.600 --> 00:16:33.510
is replicated across regions.

00:16:33.510 --> 00:16:35.440
It's sharded across regions.

00:16:35.440 --> 00:16:37.940
And it's also
sharded across zones.

00:16:37.940 --> 00:16:39.910
So what we've done
is we've basically

00:16:39.910 --> 00:16:43.120
built a hierarchy
of a control plane

00:16:43.120 --> 00:16:45.410
that manages a data plane.

00:16:45.410 --> 00:16:49.200
So first up at the
top-- and you're not

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:52.140
going to see it in this slide--
is the Cloud Cluster Manager.

00:16:52.140 --> 00:16:55.210
This performs a lot of tasks,
including configuring storage.

00:16:55.210 --> 00:16:57.420
It also configures
compute and networking.

00:16:57.420 --> 00:17:00.510
Below that sits the
Fabric Manager, which is

00:17:00.510 --> 00:17:05.310
a regional level control plane.

00:17:05.310 --> 00:17:07.020
And that comprises
a high level API

00:17:07.020 --> 00:17:09.990
to configure virtual
networks and other functions.

00:17:09.990 --> 00:17:12.192
And then you get
down to the OFE,

00:17:12.192 --> 00:17:13.650
which is our open
flow layer, which

00:17:13.650 --> 00:17:17.670
takes our API abstractions
and then converts those

00:17:17.670 --> 00:17:24.150
into open flow configurations
and programs those constructs

00:17:24.150 --> 00:17:25.589
directly to the switching layer.

00:17:25.589 --> 00:17:27.630
And then the
switching layer itself

00:17:27.630 --> 00:17:31.720
is a programmable software
switch based on Open vSwitch.

00:17:31.720 --> 00:17:35.037
And that's the core data
plane for data processing.

00:17:37.900 --> 00:17:42.240
So as an example
of how this works--

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:45.060
again, we talked about
internal load balancers.

00:17:45.060 --> 00:17:50.700
So this slide here is about
how we separate, first

00:17:50.700 --> 00:17:53.410
of all, the control
plane and the data plane.

00:17:53.410 --> 00:17:57.300
So the VMC and
the OFEs are where

00:17:57.300 --> 00:17:58.920
the control plane happens.

00:17:58.920 --> 00:18:04.450
And those program
the data plane.

00:18:04.450 --> 00:18:07.830
So in this case, what we've
got is control messages

00:18:07.830 --> 00:18:12.000
being exchanged between
the client VM and CFEs

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:17.820
and also with the VM controllers
and the Cluster Fabric Manager.

00:18:17.820 --> 00:18:20.250
We've got stats for
health reporting.

00:18:20.250 --> 00:18:24.410
And we've also got
health checks happening

00:18:24.410 --> 00:18:29.810
on the other end for load
balancing high availability.

00:18:29.810 --> 00:18:34.046
And then finally you've
got VM to VM traffic.

00:18:34.046 --> 00:18:35.420
Something to point
out here again

00:18:35.420 --> 00:18:37.760
is typical load balancers
have middle boxes.

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:42.480
But our approach for the
ILB, as I described before,

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:44.110
is that we built
the load balancers

00:18:44.110 --> 00:18:45.890
as distributed services.

00:18:45.890 --> 00:18:48.080
So what's happening
is these health

00:18:48.080 --> 00:18:51.230
checks are actually checking
services on backbends

00:18:51.230 --> 00:18:54.710
across multiple backends.

00:18:54.710 --> 00:18:58.970
And the client VM, in this
case, is where the load

00:18:58.970 --> 00:19:00.710
balancer itself is running.

00:19:00.710 --> 00:19:04.550
And we're programming
paths to those backends

00:19:04.550 --> 00:19:08.510
directly into each of the
host, both forward and reverse.

00:19:11.020 --> 00:19:13.867
So then we can also
extend those functions.

00:19:13.867 --> 00:19:15.700
And I think the interesting
thing about this

00:19:15.700 --> 00:19:18.911
is we can extend those functions
without changing typologies,

00:19:18.911 --> 00:19:20.660
because again, we don't
have middle boxes.

00:19:20.660 --> 00:19:25.180
So all we have to do is
configure the control plane,

00:19:25.180 --> 00:19:27.700
so that we can update
the data plane to add

00:19:27.700 --> 00:19:29.480
that new function into a path.

00:19:29.480 --> 00:19:33.490
So if we add an on-prem
situation in a VPN,

00:19:33.490 --> 00:19:36.700
it's very simple for us
to then program the flow

00:19:36.700 --> 00:19:39.720
to the VPN termination point.

00:19:39.720 --> 00:19:43.200
That programs the backends
that allow the load balancer

00:19:43.200 --> 00:19:44.830
to connect to
those backend VPNs.

00:19:44.830 --> 00:19:47.040
Again, we didn't
change any topology.

00:19:47.040 --> 00:19:51.010
This was simply a
software-driven operation.

00:19:55.420 --> 00:19:57.630
So let's talk about
network programming models.

00:19:57.630 --> 00:19:59.760
So it's interesting
when you're doing

00:19:59.760 --> 00:20:03.360
a software defined
network, you have

00:20:03.360 --> 00:20:05.130
to program all of these flows.

00:20:05.130 --> 00:20:08.400
You're not using distributed
routing technologies

00:20:08.400 --> 00:20:12.030
to enable every device to
talk to any other device.

00:20:12.030 --> 00:20:15.160
You're not using ARP,
for example, to say, OK,

00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:16.326
who has this IP address?

00:20:16.326 --> 00:20:17.700
We actually have
to install flows

00:20:17.700 --> 00:20:21.750
for every single conversation
that could possibly happen.

00:20:21.750 --> 00:20:25.080
So when we started, we started
by configuring full mesh

00:20:25.080 --> 00:20:26.430
communications.

00:20:26.430 --> 00:20:28.920
And that works really
well for small networks,

00:20:28.920 --> 00:20:31.350
but it starts to get really
ugly for big networks.

00:20:31.350 --> 00:20:32.700
And that's what this is showing.

00:20:32.700 --> 00:20:36.210
This is actually a
quadratic scaling problem.

00:20:36.210 --> 00:20:42.140
And in order to solve this, we
had to do something different.

00:20:42.140 --> 00:20:44.260
So what we did is we came
up with a concept called

00:20:44.260 --> 00:20:44.800
hoverboards.

00:20:44.800 --> 00:20:48.580
Basically hoverboards
are a software gateway.

00:20:48.580 --> 00:20:53.140
We use this to program
simple paths from every VM

00:20:53.140 --> 00:20:55.780
through that software
gateway to any other VM.

00:20:55.780 --> 00:20:57.820
So rather than
having a full mesh,

00:20:57.820 --> 00:21:01.870
you traverse the hoverboard.

00:21:01.870 --> 00:21:04.430
And we still preprogram
small networks.

00:21:04.430 --> 00:21:06.310
So small networks
still have a full mesh.

00:21:06.310 --> 00:21:08.830
Large networks have hoverboards.

00:21:08.830 --> 00:21:11.290
And all those packets go
through the hoverboards.

00:21:11.290 --> 00:21:13.360
And then if the
flows get too heavy--

00:21:13.360 --> 00:21:14.800
in other words,
if the hoverboards

00:21:14.800 --> 00:21:17.770
reach any scaling constraints--
we just offload those.

00:21:17.770 --> 00:21:20.170
We actually program
a direct flow

00:21:20.170 --> 00:21:23.006
into the client or
the two hosts that

00:21:23.006 --> 00:21:24.130
need to talk to each other.

00:21:27.380 --> 00:21:30.730
So by doing that,
this software gateway

00:21:30.730 --> 00:21:35.920
allowed us to achieve some
really staggering scaling

00:21:35.920 --> 00:21:36.930
improvements.

00:21:36.930 --> 00:21:39.370
And what we found out in
one of the design questions

00:21:39.370 --> 00:21:40.900
that we had to
ask ourselves when

00:21:40.900 --> 00:21:43.840
we built those
hoverboards is actually

00:21:43.840 --> 00:21:47.710
is this going to hurt by
putting these middle boxes

00:21:47.710 --> 00:21:49.290
in between all of this?

00:21:49.290 --> 00:21:53.170
So we get a scaling benefit from
that configuration and control

00:21:53.170 --> 00:21:56.130
plane, but are we going to
suffer on the data plane?

00:21:56.130 --> 00:21:59.560
And it turns out that
the vast majority of host

00:21:59.560 --> 00:22:01.062
never talk to each other.

00:22:01.062 --> 00:22:02.520
And then the vast
majority of hosts

00:22:02.520 --> 00:22:06.550
that actually do talk to each
other are very, very quiet.

00:22:06.550 --> 00:22:13.130
We're talking less than
100 kilobits per second.

00:22:13.130 --> 00:22:16.150
And so basically we
have a threshold that

00:22:16.150 --> 00:22:20.290
says, hey, anything that's
got a larger flow than this,

00:22:20.290 --> 00:22:22.077
we'll just offload
that to the host.

00:22:22.077 --> 00:22:23.410
So often times we get questions.

00:22:23.410 --> 00:22:26.350
So how can I ensure that
my hosts will directly

00:22:26.350 --> 00:22:29.091
talk to each other if I have a
latency sensitive application?

00:22:29.091 --> 00:22:30.840
It's really simple,
send a lot of traffic.

00:22:40.270 --> 00:22:49.270
So again, by implementing
the hoverboards

00:22:49.270 --> 00:22:51.670
we were able to improve
programming times

00:22:51.670 --> 00:22:53.860
by a factor of 37x.

00:22:53.860 --> 00:22:56.910
So that was a really,
really important achievement

00:22:56.910 --> 00:22:59.140
to scale this network
to the millions of users

00:22:59.140 --> 00:23:02.324
that we now have.

00:23:02.324 --> 00:23:04.115
We also found that we
could use hoverboards

00:23:04.115 --> 00:23:08.500
to scale, not just between
VMs, but also between zones.

00:23:08.500 --> 00:23:10.300
And so we just took
it to the next level

00:23:10.300 --> 00:23:13.360
and said, OK, well, if
we've got hoverboards

00:23:13.360 --> 00:23:16.720
inside the region
between VMs, we

00:23:16.720 --> 00:23:20.410
also know that, OK, the
communications between two

00:23:20.410 --> 00:23:24.890
hosts on different zones
is also likely to be quiet.

00:23:24.890 --> 00:23:27.130
And it turns out that
that's also the case.

00:23:27.130 --> 00:23:29.650
So what we did is
we also inserted

00:23:29.650 --> 00:23:32.290
hoverboards between zones.

00:23:32.290 --> 00:23:36.650
And so again, two hosts,
one in each different zone

00:23:36.650 --> 00:23:38.640
in the same region.

00:23:38.640 --> 00:23:42.580
The low bandwidth routes
traverse the hoverboards.

00:23:42.580 --> 00:23:43.630
We do local stats.

00:23:43.630 --> 00:23:46.420
This is also an interesting
detail of the hoverboards.

00:23:46.420 --> 00:23:49.090
You might think that the
hoverboard itself is actually

00:23:49.090 --> 00:23:54.460
measuring the size of the
bandwidth between the two

00:23:54.460 --> 00:23:55.060
hosts.

00:23:55.060 --> 00:23:58.300
But it's actually stats that
are driven from the host itself.

00:23:58.300 --> 00:24:01.240
And that's to prevent
the hoverboard from right

00:24:01.240 --> 00:24:03.340
at the moment when it
actually is suffering

00:24:03.340 --> 00:24:06.897
or it needs more
resources, we're

00:24:06.897 --> 00:24:08.230
not trying to make it more busy.

00:24:08.230 --> 00:24:10.810
We can actually just offload
that to the host itself.

00:24:10.810 --> 00:24:13.250
And the host itself can
create its own flows.

00:24:13.250 --> 00:24:17.620
And this is another benefit
of distributing our network

00:24:17.620 --> 00:24:21.770
functions across
the host themselves.

00:24:21.770 --> 00:24:27.980
So when we see those stats
and the bandwidth is high,

00:24:27.980 --> 00:24:30.920
we just go straight
host to host.

00:24:30.920 --> 00:24:33.260
So again, this works just
like inside of a zone.

00:24:33.260 --> 00:24:35.658
It's just one more
hierarchy of hoverboard.

00:24:39.010 --> 00:24:44.320
So I'm going to switch gears
here now and talk about the way

00:24:44.320 --> 00:24:47.350
that we do networking
at the host level.

00:24:47.350 --> 00:24:49.570
And one of the
things that people

00:24:49.570 --> 00:24:52.150
may find odd or at
least unique about us

00:24:52.150 --> 00:24:54.490
is that we do all
of our networking

00:24:54.490 --> 00:24:59.370
on the host level in user space.

00:24:59.370 --> 00:25:03.130
And so first of all, why
do we do this in software?

00:25:03.130 --> 00:25:06.760
And we'll get to the reasons
why we do this in user space,

00:25:06.760 --> 00:25:10.660
but the primary reason is we're
able to get native hardware

00:25:10.660 --> 00:25:12.790
performance in software.

00:25:12.790 --> 00:25:17.380
Our fastpath, which is basically
just doing packet forwarding,

00:25:17.380 --> 00:25:19.750
is able to push 3 million
packets per second

00:25:19.750 --> 00:25:23.590
with one dedicated CPU per host.

00:25:23.590 --> 00:25:25.630
And that translates to
greater than 30 gigabits

00:25:25.630 --> 00:25:29.630
per second on ideal situations.

00:25:29.630 --> 00:25:33.250
Then we also use code processors
for CPU intensive functions,

00:25:33.250 --> 00:25:36.760
like denial of service
protection, IP rewrites, etc.

00:25:36.760 --> 00:25:39.530
We'll talk more about
that in a second.

00:25:39.530 --> 00:25:42.220
And then we also have
an on-host control plan.

00:25:42.220 --> 00:25:43.900
And with an on-host
control plan,

00:25:43.900 --> 00:25:47.560
we're able to do things like
live migrations and hitless

00:25:47.560 --> 00:25:52.600
upgrades, because we can
just swap out the data plane

00:25:52.600 --> 00:25:56.050
right underneath that control
plane without losing any state.

00:25:59.210 --> 00:26:01.780
So, again, why
build in userspace?

00:26:01.780 --> 00:26:03.500
Well, there are a
lot of advantages.

00:26:03.500 --> 00:26:07.000
First of all, there's
security advantages.

00:26:07.000 --> 00:26:08.440
It runs without root.

00:26:08.440 --> 00:26:11.590
So pretty much the entire
data plane has no privileges.

00:26:11.590 --> 00:26:15.250
And secondly, we can do things
like continuous fuzzing,

00:26:15.250 --> 00:26:16.940
because the code
footprint is so small.

00:26:16.940 --> 00:26:26.200
We can also do things like
address space randomization.

00:26:26.200 --> 00:26:28.510
It also improves robustness.

00:26:28.510 --> 00:26:32.850
So unlike a kernel bug, which
can take a whole machine,

00:26:32.850 --> 00:26:36.100
a user process can go
down and come right back.

00:26:36.100 --> 00:26:37.720
And likewise,
userspace processes

00:26:37.720 --> 00:26:39.380
allow for nearly
weekly upgrades via

00:26:39.380 --> 00:26:41.970
our hitless upgrade process.

00:26:41.970 --> 00:26:44.180
Live migrations again
also allow for VMs

00:26:44.180 --> 00:26:46.270
to be migrated
between physical hosts

00:26:46.270 --> 00:26:49.807
without disruption, enabling
transparent host maintenance.

00:26:54.190 --> 00:26:58.040
So it also provides a
flexible architecture.

00:26:58.040 --> 00:27:00.580
So our data plane is
fully programmable.

00:27:00.580 --> 00:27:02.920
The control plane
basically programs

00:27:02.920 --> 00:27:04.930
open flow rules and
the data plane itself.

00:27:04.930 --> 00:27:06.766
It's pretty simple.

00:27:06.766 --> 00:27:08.140
The example on
this side, we have

00:27:08.140 --> 00:27:12.510
three tables; N cap, D cap, and
maybe some special functions.

00:27:12.510 --> 00:27:15.080
This all programs
the data plane,

00:27:15.080 --> 00:27:18.250
which is a key action
base data plane.

00:27:18.250 --> 00:27:21.310
And we've also enhanced
the overall vSwitch layer

00:27:21.310 --> 00:27:24.430
to include things like
stateful load balancing, which

00:27:24.430 --> 00:27:28.030
aren't available in your
standard implementation

00:27:28.030 --> 00:27:33.790
of the vSwitch D. It also does
different features for us.

00:27:33.790 --> 00:27:35.830
We've created custom
features for ourselves,

00:27:35.830 --> 00:27:37.420
like stats and billing.

00:27:37.420 --> 00:27:41.540
And we also have the ability
to do policy enforcement.

00:27:41.540 --> 00:27:44.910
These network functions
are also applied directly

00:27:44.910 --> 00:27:47.470
into the data plane
via code processors.

00:27:47.470 --> 00:27:50.680
So again, coprocessors
outside of the fastpath,

00:27:50.680 --> 00:27:52.510
which again, the
fastpath is primarily

00:27:52.510 --> 00:27:54.170
just doing forwarding.

00:27:54.170 --> 00:27:56.500
These functions
outside the fastpath

00:27:56.500 --> 00:27:58.360
include things like
traffic shaping,

00:27:58.360 --> 00:28:01.720
denial of service protection,
abuse protection, IP address

00:28:01.720 --> 00:28:02.600
rewrites.

00:28:02.600 --> 00:28:05.680
So for example, when you're
trying to send packets

00:28:05.680 --> 00:28:08.470
to the internet, you have to do
a rewrite from that private IP

00:28:08.470 --> 00:28:10.600
address to a public IP address.

00:28:10.600 --> 00:28:12.250
All of that happens
in the coprocessor.

00:28:14.760 --> 00:28:20.760
So a little bit of history
about our CPU efficiency

00:28:20.760 --> 00:28:24.274
gains as we've
improved Andromeda.

00:28:26.940 --> 00:28:29.990
First of all, when
we built this stuff,

00:28:29.990 --> 00:28:31.730
it became very,
very evident that we

00:28:31.730 --> 00:28:34.040
needed to minimize the
host and guest network CPU

00:28:34.040 --> 00:28:35.810
cycles per byte.

00:28:35.810 --> 00:28:40.010
And the way that we
did that was over

00:28:40.010 --> 00:28:47.060
time moving off of
the host completely

00:28:47.060 --> 00:28:51.260
and bypassing as much of
the guest as possible.

00:28:51.260 --> 00:28:55.160
So in the Andromeda
1.0 implementation,

00:28:55.160 --> 00:28:59.060
there was a kernel Open vSwitch
and the Open vSwitch control

00:28:59.060 --> 00:29:01.420
plane on top and
then a hypervisor.

00:29:01.420 --> 00:29:03.440
The kernel data path
only had support

00:29:03.440 --> 00:29:06.350
for egress data offload,
but it didn't have support

00:29:06.350 --> 00:29:07.940
for ingress data offload.

00:29:07.940 --> 00:29:10.100
As a consequence, you can
see that that resulted

00:29:10.100 --> 00:29:14.020
in a lot of host CPU cycles.

00:29:14.020 --> 00:29:17.040
Andromeda 1.5 optimized
the host data path.

00:29:17.040 --> 00:29:24.870
It added ingress offload
support, but still maintained

00:29:24.870 --> 00:29:26.660
the kernel data path.

00:29:26.660 --> 00:29:29.390
This optimization along
with the introduction of

00:29:29.390 --> 00:29:33.260
receive offload support improved
both the guest and the host CPU

00:29:33.260 --> 00:29:37.820
performance, and it also
allowed for live migrations,

00:29:37.820 --> 00:29:39.750
which was a pretty big deal.

00:29:39.750 --> 00:29:43.460
We couldn't do that before
we had ingress support.

00:29:43.460 --> 00:29:46.640
But the real big evolution
came with Andromeda 2.0.

00:29:46.640 --> 00:29:48.440
There, the entire
data plan was moved

00:29:48.440 --> 00:29:52.520
into user space, where we still
required one thread of handoff.

00:29:52.520 --> 00:29:56.540
So there was still a
software virtual NIC

00:29:56.540 --> 00:29:58.560
that we had to talk to.

00:29:58.560 --> 00:30:02.082
And so there was there
was a contact switch.

00:30:02.082 --> 00:30:04.540
But as you can see, there was
still a significant reduction

00:30:04.540 --> 00:30:05.780
in cycles per byte.

00:30:05.780 --> 00:30:08.660
With 2.1, we moved that
one thread handoff.

00:30:08.660 --> 00:30:10.370
And with 2.2,
we've added support

00:30:10.370 --> 00:30:12.080
for memory copy offloads.

00:30:12.080 --> 00:30:16.430
And that's led to a decrease
in more than 50 cycles per byte

00:30:16.430 --> 00:30:22.330
since we started
with Andromeda 1.0.

00:30:22.330 --> 00:30:24.910
So some throughput numbers.

00:30:24.910 --> 00:30:27.760
On a single core
per host data plane,

00:30:27.760 --> 00:30:33.400
again, the fastpath uses one
dedicated CPU on each host.

00:30:33.400 --> 00:30:35.410
On one single core
per host for our data

00:30:35.410 --> 00:30:39.370
pane on Skylake testbed
hosts, both hosts

00:30:39.370 --> 00:30:42.200
connected to the same
Top of Rack switch.

00:30:42.200 --> 00:30:44.770
We're able to get 38 gigabits
per second throughput.

00:30:44.770 --> 00:30:47.500
Again, this is ideal
situation scenarios.

00:30:47.500 --> 00:30:50.060
We don't support 38
gigabits per second in VMs.

00:30:53.310 --> 00:30:54.930
All right, you're laughing.

00:30:54.930 --> 00:30:57.450
I have something for you.

00:30:57.450 --> 00:31:00.880
So anyway, we also
noticed some VM

00:31:00.880 --> 00:31:04.260
to VM round trip time latency.

00:31:04.260 --> 00:31:06.120
So again, with the
single core per host

00:31:06.120 --> 00:31:10.980
and the same Top of Rack switch
on Skylake infrastructure,

00:31:10.980 --> 00:31:14.220
we had normal TCP
round trip time

00:31:14.220 --> 00:31:17.880
in the order of 25 milliseconds
with our TCP busy polling.

00:31:17.880 --> 00:31:21.490
So we're not doing interrupt
driven networking anymore.

00:31:21.490 --> 00:31:23.860
We were able to lower that
down to 15 milliseconds.

00:31:23.860 --> 00:31:26.160
And with DPDK, we're
able to lower that

00:31:26.160 --> 00:31:30.300
below 10 milliseconds.

00:31:30.300 --> 00:31:33.080
All right, so a quick summary.

00:31:33.080 --> 00:31:36.130
So what we talked about
today, global network.

00:31:36.130 --> 00:31:37.960
Why is a global
network important?

00:31:37.960 --> 00:31:39.400
It's easier to deploy.

00:31:39.400 --> 00:31:41.590
You've got fewer things to do.

00:31:41.590 --> 00:31:43.420
It's more reliable.

00:31:43.420 --> 00:31:46.160
The infrastructure that we
built is heavily redundant.

00:31:46.160 --> 00:31:47.100
It's more flexible.

00:31:47.100 --> 00:31:50.230
You have the ability
to operate functions

00:31:50.230 --> 00:31:53.770
both globally and locally.

00:31:53.770 --> 00:31:55.090
We talked about Andromeda.

00:31:55.090 --> 00:31:56.980
The key principles
between Andromeda

00:31:56.980 --> 00:32:00.610
are isolation,
performance, and agility.

00:32:00.610 --> 00:32:04.060
So isolation, again, we've
got performance and functional

00:32:04.060 --> 00:32:04.730
isolation.

00:32:04.730 --> 00:32:10.600
We have to keep all of these
network name spaces distinct.

00:32:10.600 --> 00:32:16.800
And agility, we need to be
able to improve as we build.

00:32:16.800 --> 00:32:19.840
And so this agility that
we've built into this

00:32:19.840 --> 00:32:22.510
allows us to do weekly upgrades.

00:32:22.510 --> 00:32:24.380
And then finally, some
network functions.

00:32:24.380 --> 00:32:28.210
And by the way, this is by
no means complete-- uh, oh,

00:32:28.210 --> 00:32:30.290
I gave away the ghost--

00:32:30.290 --> 00:32:33.400
so anyway, these
network functions

00:32:33.400 --> 00:32:36.664
are by no means a
comprehensive list.

00:32:36.664 --> 00:32:38.830
Hopefully, you've been
spending some time with this,

00:32:38.830 --> 00:32:42.190
and you know that there are
many global network functions,

00:32:42.190 --> 00:32:44.200
there are many regional
network functions.

00:32:44.200 --> 00:32:45.895
We only talked about a few.

00:32:45.895 --> 00:32:47.770
And then I have one more
thing for those that

00:32:47.770 --> 00:32:49.030
were snickering a little bit.

00:32:49.030 --> 00:32:52.030
We are announcing
today that we support

00:32:52.030 --> 00:32:55.300
32 gigabit per second
networking host to host.

00:32:57.910 --> 00:33:01.090
The way that we talk about
networking in our cloud

00:33:01.090 --> 00:33:04.880
is a general rule of thumb is
that you get about 2 gigabits

00:33:04.880 --> 00:33:07.190
per second per vCPU.

00:33:07.190 --> 00:33:12.660
So up until today, that scaled
up to 16 gigabits per second.

00:33:12.660 --> 00:33:19.320
So on an 8 vCPU box, you could
get 16 gigabits per second.

00:33:19.320 --> 00:33:21.790
Now, this requires
the Skylake platform.

00:33:21.790 --> 00:33:26.210
However, with a 16
CPU Skylake platform,

00:33:26.210 --> 00:33:29.400
you can get up to 32
gigabits per second.

00:33:29.400 --> 00:33:31.290
It requires zero configuration.

00:33:31.290 --> 00:33:36.380
There's no SR-IOV required,
no different drivers.

00:33:36.380 --> 00:33:39.830
You can use a standard virtIO
driver and this will just work.

00:33:42.830 --> 00:33:44.810
And with that,
I'd like to invite

00:33:44.810 --> 00:33:51.050
you to go to the rest of
our networking breakouts

00:33:51.050 --> 00:33:53.370
for the rest of the week.

00:33:53.370 --> 00:33:56.010
There's an interesting
hybrid one tomorrow

00:33:56.010 --> 00:33:57.710
that one of my
colleagues is giving.

00:33:57.710 --> 00:34:00.710
And also, again,
9:00 AM tomorrow,

00:34:00.710 --> 00:34:06.050
we have an IP addressing in the
cloud panel, which I think--

00:34:06.050 --> 00:34:07.520
you know, I'm a
nerd, but I think

00:34:07.520 --> 00:34:10.520
this is a really cool topic.

00:34:10.520 --> 00:34:14.920
And then if you want to cloud
poster, go to g.co/poster,

00:34:14.920 --> 00:34:18.840
where you can download one
or we will ship it to you.

00:34:18.840 --> 00:34:20.800
We're done.

